      "writtenOn": "2026-05-05T17:18:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Here is an example generated by Gemini (double-check before using):\n\n----\n\nHere is exactly how you would replace the `#define` block at the top of `util/elan/iapcontrol.cc` using this approach, along with an example of how much cleaner the function implementations become.\n\n### 1. The Definitions (Top of `iapcontrol.cc`)\n\nPlace this right after your `#include` directives. The anonymous namespace (`namespace { ... }`) ensures these variables are only visible inside this specific `.cc` file.\n\n```cpp\nnamespace ElanApp {\nnamespace {\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------\n// Internal Constants\n// ---------------------------------------------------------\nconstexpr bool kDebugLog \u003d false;\nconstexpr size_t kCountInterface \u003d 4;\nconstexpr size_t kBootPageSize \u003d 64;\nconstexpr int kTimeoutTransferMs \u003d 1000;\nconstexpr uint8_t kGoogleFwVerPrefix \u003d 0x10;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------\n// Endpoints \u0026 Lengths\n// ---------------------------------------------------------\nconstexpr uint8_t kEp1Addr \u003d 0x01;\nconstexpr uint8_t kEp2Addr \u003d 0x82;\nconstexpr uint8_t kEp3Addr \u003d 0x03;\nconstexpr uint8_t kEp4Addr \u003d 0x84;\n\nconstexpr size_t kEp1Length \u003d 8;\nconstexpr size_t kEp2Length \u003d 8;\nconstexpr size_t kEp3Length \u003d 64;\nconstexpr size_t kEp4Length \u003d 64;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------\n// Command Codes\n// ---------------------------------------------------------\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdRunIap            \u003d 0x20;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdFinishedIap       \u003d 0x21;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdSoftwareReset     \u003d 0x22;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdGetFwVersion      \u003d 0x41;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdAbortProcess      \u003d 0x48;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdWriteRom          \u003d 0xA0;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdWriteRomFinish    \u003d 0xA1;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdWriteOption       \u003d 0xA2;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdWriteOptionFinish \u003d 0xA3;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdSetRomAddress     \u003d 0xA4;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdSetOptionAddress  \u003d 0xA5;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdReadRom           \u003d 0xE0;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdReadRomFinish     \u003d 0xE1;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdReadOption        \u003d 0xE2;\nconstexpr uint8_t kCmdReadOptionFinish  \u003d 0xE3;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------\n// Status Codes\n// ---------------------------------------------------------\nconstexpr uint8_t kErrStaReady   \u003d 0x00;\nconstexpr uint8_t kErrStaSuccess \u003d 0x01;\n\n} // namespace\n\n// ... Rest of your IapControl implementation starts here ...\n```\n\n### 2. The Implementation\n\nHere is how one of your functions (`softwareReset`) looks when using these new constants. Notice that because the constants are explicitly `uint8_t` and `size_t`, you can drop them directly into the arrays and buffer assignments without any casting. \n\n*(I also included the zero-initialization fix `[kEp1Length] \u003d {}` here to remove the need for `memset`!)*\n\n```cpp\nint IapControl::softwareReset()\n{\n\t// Arrays are sized using the constexpr size_t and zero-initialized.\n\t// No memset required.\n\tuint8_t dataSend[kEp1Length] \u003d {}; \n\tint result \u003d 0;\n\n\t// No casting required because kCmdSoftwareReset is already a uint8_t.\n\tdataSend[0] \u003d kCmdSoftwareReset;\n\n\tresult \u003d iapCommand(dataSend);\n\tif (result) {\n\t\tlogInfo(\"[IAP] {} - command fail\\n\", __func__);\n\t\treturn -1;\n\t}\n\n\treturn 0;\n}\n```\n\nAnd in your status-checking logic (`iapCommand`), it looks like this:\n\n```cpp\n\t// Checking against the uint8_t command code\n\tif (statRecv[0] !\u003d buff[0]) {\n\t\tlogInfo(\"[IAP] {} - return command code does not match (0x{:02X})\\n\",\n\t\t\t__func__, statRecv[0]);\n\t\treturn -1;\n\t}\n\n\t// Checking against the uint8_t status code\n\tif (statRecv[1] !\u003d kErrStaSuccess) {\n\t\tauto debugVal \u003d get32Le(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e(statRecv + 4, 4));\n\t\tauto debugStr \u003d debugVal ? std::format(\"0x{:08X}\", *debugVal) : \"N/A\";\n\n\t\tlogInfo(\"[IAP] {} - fail, error code: 0x{:02X}, value: {}\\n\",\n\t\t\t__func__, statRecv[1], debugStr);\n\t\treturn -1;\n\t}\n```",
